  "corrections": [
    "See docs/audit-security-web.json for exact original/corrected text, reasons and per-file coverage.",
    "CORS credentials/method/header/opaque-origin semantics, preflight cache and Vary preservation corrected.",
    "XSS category overlap, escaping contexts, React19 URL behavior, strict-dynamic and Trusted Types limitations corrected.",
    "CSRF Unicode token failure, client-side CSRF scope, signed double-submit/session binding, SameSite prerequisites, metadata limits and clickjacking fixed.",
    "retrofit: web-csrf safe methods listed GET, HEAD, OPTIONS only -> now GET, HEAD, OPTIONS and TRACE (RFC 9110 9.2.1).",
    "retrofit: glossary coep said COEP loads cross-origin resources only if they opt in -> now distinguishes require-corp (CORP/same-origin) from credentialless (no CORP needed, sent without cookies).",
    "retrofit: web-xss-q6 \"React 19 blocks javascript: URLs\" made precise: React 19 replaces them with a javascript: URL that only throws (sanitizeURL.js, v19.0.0).",
    "retrofit: wrong-sense links reworded: forge/forged/forging (signature forgery) -> fake/faked; exploits (kill-chain exploitation) -> abuses; verify the signature (digital signature) -> verify the HMAC; metadata -> Fetch Metadata headers; Authorization bearer token -> bearer token in the Authorization header; evict/eviction (IR eradication) and retention (log retention) -> dropped/kept; Persistence (malware) -> where the payload lives; request-context signal (Signal app) -> indicator; Django docs link text hyphenated.",
    "retrofit: glossary aliases narrowed to stop cross-module/cross-subject wrong senses: removed sanitiser/sanitizer (compiler sanitizers in security/05), GETs, constant time, hash policy, signed token, session store, event handler(s), rich text/rich-text, default policy; added HTML sanitizer and Trusted Types default policy; lesson text reworded to keep links (hash-based CSP, default Trusted Types policy, Event-handler attributes, HTML sanitiser).",
    "retrofit: glossary escape-hatch-template generalised (term \"Escape hatch\") so cross-subject fallback in languages/software-engineering reads correctly; pen-tester definition now also defines penetration test."
  ],
